// A tail-position select whose SCRUTINEE stands bottom returned a
// FRESH bottom on every invocation, where the node-walk returns a
// STANDING one.
//
// `emit_tail_select` (fusion/emit/flow.rs ~398) branches a tainted
// scrutinee straight to `emit_kernel_return` with an
// `emit_elem_placeholder` — and that placeholder's disc is TAINT with
// no STALE, i.e. a fresh bottom, whatever the scrutinee's own trigger
// was. The value-position twin gets this right: `emit_select_bottom_
// value` ORs the scrutinee's `stale_bits` into the trap disc.
//
// Here `1 / 0` is a fresh bottom at init and a STANDING one forever
// after, so `f(1)` is a fresh bottom at init and quiet afterwards.
// `v` is a `<-` target, so the difference is observable: a FRESH
// production re-publishes the binding, and the republish lands in the
// same cycle as the connect's queued write and clobbers it.
//
//   interp: `v` publishes bottom at init, stays quiet, and the connect
//           delivers -> [1:i64:1]
//   jit:    `v` re-publishes a fresh bottom every cycle, so the write
//           never survives -> []
//
// The non-tail twin (`|n| select 1 / 0 { m => m }`) always agreed —
// only the tail-position return path mints the placeholder.
{
    let rec f = |n: i64| -> i64 select 1 / 0 { m => f(m) };
    let v = f(1);
    v <- 1;
    v
}
